      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 16104.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 783,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the fundamental prerequisite for life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of water in all its fascinating dimensions:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Unique Physical Properties of Water\nWater is weird. By the rules of chemistry, it should be a gas at room temperature (just like hydrogen sulfide, which has a similar structure). Instead, because of how its molecules bond together (via *hydrogen bonding*), water has unique properties that make life possible:\n\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (oceans, rivers), **solid** (ice, snow), and **gas** (steam, water vapor).\n* **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats on top of lakes, allowing fish and aquatic life to survive the winter underneath.\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as nature's transport system, carrying chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nEarth is often called the \"Blue Planet\" because water covers about **71% of its surface**. \n* **The Oceans:** About 96.5% of all Earth's water is held in the oceans as salt water.\n* **Freshwater:** Only about 2.5% of Earth's water is fresh. \n* **Availability:** Most of that freshwater is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n\nWater is constantly moving in a giant loop known as the **water cycle** (or hydrological cycle): it evaporates from the oceans into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow, and flows through rivers back to the sea.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nBiology cannot exist without water. \n* **Inside Us:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are roughly 31% water.\n* **Cellular Function:** Water is the medium in which all the cellular processes of life take place. It flushes out toxins, regulates body temperature, cushions joints, and transports nutrients through the blood.\n* **Origin:** Scientists believe life itself originally sparked in Earth’s primordial oceans billions of years ago, protected from harsh solar radiation by a watery shield.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with miraculous properties. It shapes our geography, drives our weather, sustains every living ecosystem, and flows through our own veins. Without it, Earth would be a lifeless, barren rock."
